- Samplers should be designed appropriately to ensure a full "cut" of each stream can be performed without spillage; 

- Ideally, sample as many cyclones as possible; 

- Each sample cut should be performed as close as possible to the overflow and underflow outlet streams; 

- Conduct sampling over a 1-2 hour period, with sample cuts taken every 15-30 minutes; and 

- Cyclone feed is usually sampled by blanking off a cyclone overflow, or from a bleed pipe off the cyclone feed distributor. Take this sample at the end of the survey to minimise process upsets caused by opening and closing cyclones and sample points. 

###### **Cyclone assessment** 

Once survey samples are collected, the per cent solids and particle size distributions of each sample should be measured and mass balanced in conjunction with any available online instrumentation data (e.g. volumetric flowrates, water additions). Once this is performed, cyclone efficiency curves can be constructed by calculating the per cent of each size fraction reporting to the cyclone overflow. This should be plotted against log-scale screen size as shown in Figure 3. Efficiency curves are well explained in the JKMRC “Blue Book” (Napier-Munn et al, 1999, pp 276-285). 



Figure 3 – Example cyclone efficiency curves. 

The shape of these efficiency curves is controlled by parameters that reflect the performance of the cyclones. These can be calculated easily using a software package such as JKSimMet, or even in Excel: 

- D50 – the cut size; 

- Alpha - sharpness of efficiency curve; and 

- Water split to overflow (per cent). 

Alpha represents the steepness of the efficiency slope and is largely a reflection of the cyclones’ ability to prevent coarse particles from escaping to the overflow at a given cut-size. Typical alpha values range between 1-3. 

As a general guide, average to good performing cyclones will recover 60 to 70 per cent water to the overflow, removing a commensurate amount of fines from the circuit. Cyclones that do this well alleviate recirculating loads in the ball milling circuit, in turn allowing a finer grind size to be achieved or, alternatively, throughput to be increased. 

