###### **WHAT NOT TO DO** 

JKTech over the years has observed numerous issues relating to cyclone operation and monitoring. Sometimes this is due to engineering companies not accounting for operational aspects in their designs. A recently constructed plant in Australia had their cyclone underflow launder installed at least three metres above the floor grating, requiring a large step ladder to observe the cyclone underflow. The following points should be considered; 

1. Do not disregard the importance of cyclone inspections. Lack of maintenance checks performed by a maintenance technician and/or metallurgist can result in poor classification a

hether they are underloaded, overloaded or operating correctly. This should be performed routinely through the day; 

- Check that the internal components have not worn excessively, e.g. spigots and vortex finders etc, at each major shutdown. A quick inspection of the apex and spigots should be undertaken for measurements, wear, blockages etc; 

- Monitor the control of cyclones to ensure the correct pressure and density targets are being met, as well as the variation of these parameters; 

- Ensure cyclone run/operating hours are recorded so that essential maintenance is performed when required, before cyclone failure; 

- Conduct density checks of the cyclone streams, ideally the feed and overflow with an appropriate solids SG check. Then pour the cyclone overflow sample over a relatively coarse sieve/screen to check for unwanted large particles, this will indicate whether a cyclone survey should be performed; 

- Routinely perform cyclone surveys to monitor the efficiency of classification to provide valuable benchmarking data. The rewards could be large; and 

- Perform simulations of alternative internal components that could improve performance. Correct use of JKSimMet can provide robust guidance for improvement initiates without undergoing long, extensive trials of multiple components. 

